\begin_layout Standard
Consider a growth model with foreign aid:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $\begin{array}{cl}
\mbox{max} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})}\\
s.t. & c_{t}+k_{t+1}\le F(k_{t},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}+a\\
 & k_{0}>0
\end{array}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $a>0$
\end_inset

 denotes the foreign aid.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{ss}(a)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{ss}(a)$
\end_inset

 be the capital stock and consumption in the steady state.
 Determine 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dk_{ss}(a)}{da}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dc_{ss}(a)}{da}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k_{t})=F(k_{t},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $L=1$
\end_inset

 is a constant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime}(k_{t})=F_{k}(k_{t},1)+(1-\delta)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The Lagrangian:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})}+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\lambda_{t}(f(k_{t})+a-c_{t}-k_{t+1})}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First order conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial L}{\partial c_{t}}=\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})-\lambda_{t}=0\Longleftrightarrow\lambda_{t}=\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial L}{\partial k_{t+1}}=-\lambda_{t}+\lambda_{t+1}f^{\prime}(k_{t+1})=0\Longleftrightarrow\lambda_{t}=\lambda_{t+1}f^{\prime}\left(k_{t+1}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Complementary slackness condition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\lambda_{t}(f(k_{t})+a-c_{t}-k_{t+1})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the optimal point, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}>0\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k_{t})+a-c_{t}-k_{t+1}=0\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

 from the complementary slackness condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})=\beta^{t+1}u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})f^{\prime}\left(k_{t+1}\right)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

by the first two first order conditions above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Euler Equation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align left
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}\left(c_{t}\right)=\beta u^{\prime}\left(c_{t+1}\right)f^{\prime}\left(k_{t+1}\right)\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Find capital stock and consumption in the steady state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the steady state, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=c_{ss}\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}=k_{ss}\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, the Euler Equation becomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}\left(c_{ss}\right)=\beta u^{\prime}\left(c_{ss}\right)f^{\prime}\left(k_{ss}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\iff\frac{1}{\beta}=f^{\prime}\left(k_{ss}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\iff k_{ss}=\left(f^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, in the steady state, the feasability constraint becomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $c_{ss}+k_{ss}=f\left(k_{ss}\right)+a$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\iff c_{ss}=f\left(k_{ss}\right)+a-k_{ss}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Determine 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dk_{ss}(a)}{da}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dc_{ss}(a)}{da}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{ss}$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 then,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dk_{ss}(a)}{da}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, taking the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{ss}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

, we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dc_{ss}(a)}{da}=\frac{\partial f(k_{ss})}{\partial k_{ss}}\cdot\frac{dk_{ss}}{da}+1-\frac{dk_{ss}}{da}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But since 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dk_{ss}(a)}{da}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dc_{ss}(a)}{da}=1$
\end_inset


